Over the past few months I’ve been working on rewriting Traction Point in C3 . All the Zig code in the game has been replaced with C3, and the modding SDK will follow when the next playtest version is released. Why, you ask? Well, ever since I made it possible to hot-reload the game code, it has become an integral part of my workflow for developing the game. Want to tweak a UI element? Make the change and hot-reload the code while the game is running, and it shows up. An animation needing a longer ease-in? Hot-reload and you will see it in seconds. Need a temporary print in the code to isolate a bug in the state of the game? Hot-reload.

The problem with Zig is that building code on Windows is sloooow . These days Zig has a self-hosted backend which compiles code relatively quickly, at least in debug mode, but unfortunately it’s not yet available on Windows. They even added a form of incremental-compilation, but it doesn’t remove the long LLVM linking times. To give you an idea of what I am talking about, a full build of the Traction Point debug configuration, at a little over 100k LoC of Zig, takes around 24s . Changing a single line and rebuilding, with a warm cache, takes over 15s . C3 compiles the full game, without caching, in about 3s . I’ve seen it be as fast as 2.6s . Now, this is podracing! It will probably get a bit slower before the game is done, as more code is added, but I am sure you can see the benefits here.

C3 is not as widely known as other modern systems programming languages, but it fits the project perfectly. I wanted a language that could act almost as a drop-in replacement for Zig. That meant the memory model needed to be the same (no GC, no RAII), it needed to compile to WASM (for mod support), it needed to be fast (obviously, we are making a game here), and it needed to compile quickly. Preferrably it also would be somewhat easy for other people to pick up, and while I am personally quite fond of the Zig syntax, going with a C-derived language like C3 makes a lot of sense for attracting modders in the future.

Q: But what about Odin, Rust, Jai or HotNewLanguage™?

A: Yes, some of those might have fit the bill too, but I went with C3.

For my purposes, Zig and C3 were similar enough that almost all of the concepts used in the code could be ported over one-to-one, and I could do an almost mechanical translation of the code, which I think was the only way this port was ever going to happen. Let’s see what the new code looks like, by grabbing a few pieces from a random game component:

struct WireRendererComponent @Component ( "means.WireRendererComponent" )

WireRendererGeometryMode geometryMode @Exposed ( WireRendererGeometryMode . CURVE_PATH_3D );

AllocString curveDataBlockName @Exposed ( "" );

uint curveSampleResolution @Exposed ( 16 );

AllocString point0Object @Exposed ( "" );

AllocString point1Object @Exposed ( "" );

AllocString materialRef @ExposedResourceRef ( ResourceTypeID . MATERIAL , "" );

fn void WireRendererComponent . createVisualCurvePath (& self )

RendererPtr renderer = self . context . getRenderer ();

GameObjectPtr gameObject = self . context . getGameObject ();

self . pointCount = self . curveSampleResolution ;

MeshGeometryPtr geometry = mesh_geometry :: newGeometry ();

MeshGeometryLodLevelPtr lod0 = geometry . addLodLevel ();

MeshGeometrySubMeshPtr subMesh = lod0 . addSubMesh ( VERTEX_FORMAT_TYPE );

self . generateIndicesCurvePath ( subMesh );

self . generateGeometryCurvePath ( subMesh );

. kind = MeshSourceKind . MESH_GEOMETRY_IMMUTABLE ,

C3 supports annotations, both built-in and user-specified, and we are using a few above. Tagging a struct with @Component

